From 6b3ac5c4d87e2a26b55a088f65aaffa363df3e41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 12:06:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/20] fix(operator): the owner's command carries --sponsored
 only where the paymaster sponsors it

When the Safe lacks the owner's grant, `jinn signer-migrate` prints the owner's root command.
With `signer.sponsored` in the config it added --sponsored without checking that the paymaster
would sponsor that operation. A point of #250 in the agent-keys repository (B3).

- signer/migrate.ts: rootOperationGas sizes the sponsored root operation of one call as
  agent-signer sizes it before a bundler is asked (agent-keys signer/crates/agent-signer/src/
  chain.rs, `send_raw_at` and `estimate_call_gas`): verificationGasLimit 300000, or 1500000 for
  the operation that deploys the account; callGasLimit from eth_estimateGas of the Kernel's
  `execute` called by the EntryPoint, plus 30%, at least 150000, or, before the account has
  code, of the call from the account's address plus 40000, plus 30%, at least 300000;
  preVerificationGas 100000; the paymaster's verification gas 100000 and no postOp;
  maxFeePerGas as alloy estimates it from the node (twice the next block's base fee plus the
  median of the last 10 blocks' 20th-percentile priority fees above zero, at least 1 wei). Its
  maxCost is what the EntryPoint passes the paymaster. rootOperationSponsorship reads the
  WhitelistPaymaster's sponsorships(account) and says the operation is sponsored only when
  maxCost fits both the per-operation cap and the budget left, which the paymaster checks at
  validation; a paymaster that does not answer, or a gas estimate that fails, is a reason not to.
  Step 1 prints the command with --sponsored and why when it is sponsored, and otherwise without
  it and the reason. whitelistPaymasterOf takes the paymaster from the agent config's
  deployments file (`whitelistPaymaster`, the file resolved from the config's directory, as
  agent-signer reads it), or says why none is known; readAgentConfig reads `deployments`.
  A bundler's estimate can replace agent-signer's figures, bounded at twice each (its fee at
  twice the node's): with a bundler this is the figure that bound starts from, not a ceiling.
- cli/commands/signer-migrate.ts: the start of the printed command no longer carries
  --sponsored; with `signer.sponsored` the migration is given the paymaster; the help says so.
- tests: unit: within the cap and the budget left the command carries --sponsored and the cost
  (for an account with code and one without); above the cap, above the budget left, a paymaster
  that does not answer and a deployments file without a paymaster each print it without
  --sponsored, with the reason; without signer.sponsored nothing is added; the paymaster is read
  from the deployments file relative to the agent config. In the full loop, the migration's
  sizing of the owner's grant is compared with the operation agent-signer sends (no bundler
  there): verificationGasLimit, callGasLimit, preVerificationGas and maxFeePerGas are the same.
  Each rule was broken once on purpose and a test caught it (the cap, the budget, --sponsored
  printed regardless, the first operation's call floor, the fee's multiplier, the paymaster's
  gas).

